### Apply settings at startup
In `EmuWin` after machine creation:
```
ApplyAudioSettings();
```
Add private method:
```
private void ApplyAudioSettings()
{
 if (machine is null) return;
 machine.Sid.OutputVolume = AppSettings.Current.AudioVolume;
 machine.Sid.Muted = AppSettings.Current.AudioMuted;
}
```
Could avoid method and inline; method useful if menu setting changes? Menu directly sets SID. Maybe Menu can call `machine.Sid.OutputVolume` and `Muted` but no access to `AppSettings`? It has `using System`; can use. Better centralize audio settings in a class? Let's think architecture.

Options:
A) `AppSettings` holds values; Menu applies to `machine.Sid`; EmuWin applies initial. Duplicated mapping.
B) Add `SID.ConfigureOutput(int volume, bool muted)` and call from both. Still mapping.
C) Add an `AudioSettings` service? overkill.
D) Add `C64Machine.AudioVolume` and `AudioMuted` properties proxying SID; menu and EmuWin use. This is clean:
```
public int AudioVolume { get => Sid.OutputVolume; set => Sid.OutputVolume=value; }
public bool AudioMuted { ... }
```
But SID itself could expose; no need.
Maybe `SID` properties `OutputVolume`, `Muted`; EmuWin and Menu apply. Simple.

Menu can call:
```
private void SetVolume(int value) { ... machine.Sid.OutputVolume=value; }
```
EmuWin initial sets. Fine.

### UI text and behavior
"Volume: 80%" with left/right adjusting 5. Enter should maybe increase; intuitive? Existing `fps` uses left/right only; Enter on "Display FPS" currently does nothing because no case "fps"; but `fps.left/right` toggle. For volume, users may press Enter expecting toggle; could support `"volume"` increments +5. But menu instructions not shown; left/right likely expected. Add case "volume" -> +5 (or maybe reset default). Mute toggles on Enter. We can add `case "volume": case "volume.right":` and `left`. That means pressing Enter on volume increases; okay.
- For mute, `"mute"`, `"mute.left"`, `"mute.right"` toggle.
- Text refresh helper:
```
private void UpdateAudioMenu()
{
 if (menuList.Count >=3) { menuList[1].Text = ...; menuList[2].Text = ...; }
}
```
Could have `SetVolume` update text. Keep straightforward.
- When `Audio` submenu Load:
```
menuList.Add(new MenuList("Back to Main Menu", "main"));
menuList.Add(new MenuList("Volume: " + AppSettings.Current.AudioVolume + "%", "volume"));
menuList.Add(new MenuList("Mute: " + (AppSettings.Current.AudioMuted ? "Yes" : "No"), "mute"));
```
- `ProcessCommand` cases:
```
case "audio": LoadAudioMenu(); break;
case "volume":
case "volume.right":
    ChangeVolume(5); break;
case "volume.left":
    ChangeVolume(-5); break;
case "mute":
case "mute.left":
case "mute.right":
    ToggleMute(); break;
```
- `ChangeVolume`:
```
private void ChangeVolume(int delta)
{
 int volume = Math.Clamp(AppSettings.Current.AudioVolume + delta, 0, 100);
 AppSettings.Current.AudioVolume = volume;
 machine.Sid.OutputVolume = volume;
 AppSettings.Current.Save();
 menuList[1].Text = "Volume: " + volume + "%";
}
```
- Should stepping be 10%? 5% means 20 presses from 0 to 100; okay. Could 10% easier. User didn't specify. Use 5 or 10? Menu keyboard; 10% practical. But fine-grained volume better; 5%. Let's use 5.
- `ToggleMute`:
```
bool muted=!AppSettings.Current.AudioMuted;
...
machine.Sid.Muted=muted;
if (muted) machine.Sid.DiscardSamples();
...
```
Should we clear queued audio immediately? If we set `Muted`, new samples zero; queued old samples up to 75ms. To make mute instantaneous, call `machine.Sid.DiscardSamples()`. That discards SID queue, but SDL output queue still has old samples. `AudioOutput` queue can be up to 20ms; no direct access. Could expose `AudioOutput.Flush`; not worth. 20ms unnoticeable.
- When volume set to 0, should treat as muted? UI shows 0%; fine. Could `Mute` separate.
- On unmute, volume applies. 
- If setting `Muted` true, SID still generates zeros; CPU overhead minor.

### SID gain
Add:
```
private volatile int outputVolume = 100;
private volatile bool outputMuted;

public int OutputVolume
{
 get => outputVolume;
 set => outputVolume = Math.Clamp(value, 0, 100);
}
public bool Muted
{
 get => outputMuted;
 set => outputMuted = value;
}
```
Place after properties? Need avoid conflict with `volume` local in GenerateSample. Use `OutputVolume` field `outputVolume`.
In GenerateSample:
```
double hostVolume = outputMuted ? 0.0 : outputVolume / 100.0;
double output = direct * volume * MasterGain * hostVolume;
```
Should host volume perhaps default 100; EmuWin sets from AppSettings. Tests instantiate SID and expect full; default 100. Good.
- `volatile int` with Math.Clamp setter; okay. `Muted` auto? use field to be volatile:
```
private volatile bool muted;
public bool Muted { get => muted; set => muted = value; }
```
Name `OutputMuted`? `Muted` okay.
- `SID.Reset` does not reset outputVolume/muted; good.
- Since SID is core, depends on host setting but property explicit. Fine.
- Could use `Volatile.Read` in GenerateSample each sample; volatile read cheap.

