This is almost certainly **presentation cadence**, not the game’s scrolling logic.

The C64 core runs PAL at **50.125 fps** — `985248 Hz / 19656 cycles` — and [C64Machine.cs:22](C64Emulator/C64Machine.cs#L22) paces it correctly. The renderer then enables VSync unconditionally at [Renderer.cs:119](C64Emulator/Renderer.cs#L119), while [EmuWin.cs:108](C64Emulator/EmuWin.cs#L108) calls `Present()` on every host loop, even when no new VIC frame is ready.

On a typical 60 Hz display, 50.125 fps does not divide evenly into 60. Some emulated frames are held for two refreshes, producing a visible 3:2-style cadence. Vertical scrolling exposes this immediately because every row moves at once. The fullscreen mode picker also tracks only width and height, so it does not select a 50 Hz refresh mode.

The VIC is additionally an approximation: [VIC.cs:215](C64Emulator/VIC.cs#L215) advances eight pixels per CPU cycle, and raster-split scrollers can still show tearing because the VIC is not cycle-exact. But if horizontal scrolling looks smooth and vertical scrolling has a regular hitch, the 60 Hz-vs-50.125 Hz mismatch is the first thing to rule out.

I did not change it because you asked why. The targeted fix would be a VSync/50 Hz display option plus presenting only completed frames; if vertical-only jumpiness remains with VSync off, then it is VIC scroll/raster timing.
